%% The Topic 3 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 3. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig pyramid | Each wave deepened the meaning of equality.
First wave: equal before the law — the vote, property
Second wave: equal in work, family, body — "the personal is political"
Third wave: equal among women — diversity, intersectionality
Fourth wave: equal in daily life — safety, consent, #MeToo
:::

**Draw it in:** every waves question (2019, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025). Add one Pakistani echo for each wave.

::: fig timeline | The UN road for women's rights.
@layout vertical
1946: Commission on the Status of Women
1975: Mexico City — International Women's Year
1979: CEDAW (in force 1981; Pakistan 1996)
1985: Nairobi — Forward-Looking Strategies
1993: Vienna — "women's rights are human rights"
1995: Beijing — Platform for Action
2000: UNSCR 1325; MDGs
2010: UN Women
2015: SDG 5
:::

**Draw it in:** any UN question — never yet a full question, so it is due — and the background of every Pakistan question.

::: fig timeline | Pakistan's women's movement in six phases.
@layout vertical
== 1 · Welfare and reform (1947–70)
1949: APWA; 1961: MFLO
== 2 · Constitutional equality (1971–77)
1973: Articles 25, 34; reserved seats
== 3 · Resistance under Zia (1977–88)
1981: WAF; 1983: 12 February protest
== 4 · Democracy and Beijing (1988–99)
1988: Benazir Bhutto; 1996: CEDAW
== 5 · Reforms and machinery (2000–17)
2000: NCSW; 2006: Protection of Women Act; 2016: honour law
== 6 · New activism (2018–)
2018: first Aurat March
:::

**Draw it in:** the movement in Pakistan (2016, 2022, 2024, 2026) and APWA and WAF (2018).

::: fig cycle | The NGO-isation cycle, as critics describe it.
@centre A movement becomes a project
Donors set the themes
Three-year funding cycles
Activists become employees
Reports go up to donors
Women served, not organised
The street empties
:::

**Draw it in:** NGO-isation (2026). Then balance it: NGOs also drafted and defended the laws of 2010–2016.
